WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.560
Astrophysics is our gateway to understanding&nbsp;
just a fraction of the vast realm of space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.040
For the brightest young minds on our planet,&nbsp;
there's a challenge that puts their knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.160
of this realm to the test. It's the&nbsp;
International Olympiad on Astronomy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.240
Astrophysics. Let's dive into this challenge&nbsp;
together and see what we can learn from it.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.880
Once a year, the exam gathers students&nbsp;
from around the world and serves as a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:32.080
to encourage students to study astronomy and also&nbsp;
fosters international collaboration. You have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.440
be a high school student to take part, but the&nbsp;
exam covers content you would encounter well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:43.880
into a university degree on these subjects.&nbsp;
This is a copy of the 2019 exam questions.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:48.400
These questions cover such a broad range&nbsp;
of knowledge that to ace it, you need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:54.440
be superbly well-rounded in your astronomy and&nbsp;
astrophysics knowledge. I was shocked to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.440 --> 00:01:00.720
what's near the end of this paper. It's something&nbsp;
that sets this exam apart from any astronomy exam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:07.160
I've ever taken. It's not just enough here to&nbsp;
memorize some equations and plug in some numbers.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:13.320
The last page of this exam is a practical test,&nbsp;
meaning you go out at night with a telescope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:19.600
and have to find real objects in the sky under a&nbsp;
time pressure and with an examiner watching you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:25.800
That sounds intense. But before we get to that,&nbsp;
let's start with a look at the theory questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:32.640
Question one asks for this list of 11 astronomy&nbsp;
related events to be put in chronological order.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:38.560
So what do you think came first? Some options&nbsp;
include the discovery of the planet Uranus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.560
the discovery of the first planetary nebula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.440
or the first successful measurement of&nbsp;
stellar parallax. And then what was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:52.400
most recent? The discovery of the expansion of&nbsp;
the universe? Viking probes landing on Mars?

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.840
Or the launch of the Hubble Space Telescope? In&nbsp;
the answer, the first event was the discovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:03.200
of the planetary nebula, and the last event&nbsp;
was the launch of Hubble. Pause to read the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:09.480
rest of the order. Question two is about a&nbsp;
measurement made in 1919 of starlight that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:15.240
was deflected from its path by a small&nbsp;
angle as it passed by close to the sun.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:20.080
The amount of deflection is said to be&nbsp;
proportional to the mass of the sun and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:26.720
distance away that the light passes it, multiplied&nbsp;
by a few constants. The question asks if it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:33.680
be possible to detect a deflection of light that&nbsp;
passed close by Jupiter or the moon. To solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:39.480
this, you need to recognize that the distance to&nbsp;
be used in the formula is the radius of the body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:44.760
and then compare your estimated angle of&nbsp;
deflection against the present day accuracy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:52.040
this technique, which is 0.1 milliarcseconds. With&nbsp;
that accuracy, you would indeed be able to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:57.880
light deflected by Jupiter, but not from the moon,&nbsp;
as the moon is not massive enough to bend the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:04.800
light a detectable amount. In 2019, the exam was&nbsp;
held in Hungary, and you can see a local reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:12.040
in question 6. It is about European satellites,&nbsp;
named Sentinel 2, and shows two images taken by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:17.560
the satellites of a chimney in Hungary, which&nbsp;
is the third tallest building in the country.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:23.800
One image was taken in June, near the time of&nbsp;
summer solstice, when the North Pole is tilted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:30.000
closest to the sun. And one image was taken&nbsp;
in December, near the time of winter solstice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.080
where the North Pole is tilted furthest&nbsp;
away from the sun. We are asked to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:41.600
which image is which, or as they say, which&nbsp;
date do we expect the shadow to be longer?

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:47.440
See if you can get it. Would the chimney&nbsp;
have a longer shadow in June or December?

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:53.760
The answer is December. That's their winter,&nbsp;
and the sun elevation is lower in winter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:59.680
leading to longer shadows. This question continues&nbsp;
on and gets harder and much more mathematical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:05.360
when students are asked to then estimate the&nbsp;
height of the chimney using the shadow length.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.800
It isn't as simple as Pythagoras and instead&nbsp;
involves estimating the elevation angle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.360
the sun using a trig identity and solving&nbsp;
a quadratic equation. There's a link in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:21.840
description to the solutions document if you want&nbsp;
to try it yourself. Other interesting questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.520
after this one cover the effects of sunspots,&nbsp;
the changing brightness of variable stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:33.280
finding the distance to a galaxy cluster based&nbsp;
on the velocities of the galaxies inside it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.480
and seeing if it would be possible to photograph a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:41.880
Hungarian nanosatellite from the ground&nbsp;
before it burned up in the atmosphere.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:48.040
These questions are hard because they require&nbsp;
tricky conversions between units and a seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:54.360
impossible to remember array of various formulae&nbsp;
and equations. The next section of the exam is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.360 --> 00:05:00.440
more intense, as it is data analysis, with&nbsp;
questions that more closely resemble what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:06.480
research astronomy might look like, sifting&nbsp;
through observational data to find patterns.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.240
Someone who works as an astronomer would&nbsp;
likely spend their time at a computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:17.920
writing programs and processing images of stars&nbsp;
or galaxies, often taken by other people using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:24.000
huge ground based or even space telescopes.&nbsp;
A professional astronomer could get away with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.800
never operating a small backyard telescope&nbsp;
themselves, unless they wanted to for fun.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:35.120
But in this exam, that knowledge is&nbsp;
essential, and it's actually nice to see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:40.520
promoting what sometimes feels like a&nbsp;
forgotten art. In Observational Task 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:47.120
you get five minutes to align a finderscope, so&nbsp;
that what it shows in its crosshairs is the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:53.160
as what you see in the main eyepiece. Then you&nbsp;
get ten minutes to observe the planet Saturn.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:57.200
You have to find and mark the&nbsp;
position of Saturn's moon Titan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:02.400
and show the orientation of Saturn's&nbsp;
rings. I recently got a telescope again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:07.440
after years without having one, and relearning&nbsp;
how to use it brought back a lot of memories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:12.520
for me of times when I used to do this&nbsp;
often. I couldn't believe how many nights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:18.520
I had let pass without looking at Saturn, or&nbsp;
seeing the things that are right up there.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:22.080
You don't need a telescope to do&nbsp;
that, you just need to be curious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:26.640
and willing to go outside in the cold. But&nbsp;
having to fiddle around with a telescope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:31.600
forces you to be out there long enough&nbsp;
to take notice. Inspired by this exam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:38.080
I tried to complete the third task, which&nbsp;
is observing the planetary nebula M57.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:44.200
Other challenges from the exam include finding&nbsp;
a variable star in the constellation Cygnus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:50.440
and estimating the magnitude of two stars in&nbsp;
the constellation Ursa Minor just using your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:56.320
eyes. This exam has reminded me why I love&nbsp;
astronomy, but competitive exams do have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:01.680
knack for beating passion out of people&nbsp;
by cranking up the intensity and making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:07.240
the pursuit of knowledge about winning&nbsp;
rather than about joy or understanding.

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:13.240
I hope that the students who are lucky enough to&nbsp;
participate in this event are able to maintain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:18.880
and channel their enthusiasm for the subject.&nbsp;
For non students, if this stuff interests you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:24.400
then maybe think about joining a local&nbsp;
astronomy club. Whole careers can be made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:29.360
out of astronomy and astrophysics, and&nbsp;
not just in the ways you might expect.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:35.360
I'm excited to introduce the sponsor of this&nbsp;
video, a non-profit called 80000 Hours. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

